## **WEBSITE DEVELOPMENT (45 minutes) - Professional File Structure & Multi-Page Navigation**

### **Phase 1: Prepare (10 minutes)**

**Connection to Previous Learning:**
Your website currently has HTML, CSS, and JavaScript all working together, but everything might be in one file or scattered across different files without a clear organization. Today you'll learn how professional developers organize their code and create websites with multiple connected pages.

**Today's Learning Objective:**
Transform your existing single-page website into a **professional multi-page website** with **organized file structure**, **consistent navigation**, and **reusable components** like headers and footers.

**Professional Website Structure Concepts:**

**What Professional Developers Know:**
- **Separate files** for HTML, CSS, and JavaScript make code **easier to maintain**
- **Consistent navigation** helps users move between pages smoothly
- **Reusable components** (headers, footers) save time and ensure consistency
- **Organized folder structure** makes projects easier to manage and expand

**File Organization Best Practices:**

**Folder Structure:**
```
MyWebsite/
├── index.html          (Home page)
├── about.html          (About page)
├── projects.html       (Projects page)
├── contact.html        (Contact page)
├── css/
│   └── styles.css      (All CSS styles)
├── js/
│   └── script.js       (All JavaScript)
└── images/
    └── (your images)
```

**Why This Structure Works:**
- **Easy to find files** - everything has a logical location
- **Easy to maintain** - make one change to CSS, affects all pages
- **Professional standard** - matches how real websites are built
- **Scalable** - easy to add more pages later

**Multi-Page Website Components:**

**1. Navigation Menu:**
- **Same on every page** for consistency
- **Shows current page** so users know where they are
- **Links work** from any page to any other page

**2. Header Section:**
- **Consistent branding** across all pages
- **Logo or site title** in the same place
- **Main navigation menu**

**3. Footer Section:**
- **Contact information** available on every page
- **Social media links** (even if placeholder for now)
- **Copyright notice**
- **Additional navigation links**

### **Phase 2: Present (20 minutes)**

**Creating Professional Website Structure**

**Step 1: Organize Your Files**

**Current File Assessment:**
Look at your current website files and identify:
- **HTML content:** Where is it currently stored?
- **CSS styles:** Are they in a separate file or embedded in HTML?
- **JavaScript code:** Is it in a separate file or embedded in HTML?

**File Organization Process:**

**Create New Folder Structure:**
1. Create a **css** folder in your MyWebsite directory
2. Create a **js** folder in your MyWebsite directory
3. Create an **images** folder for future use

**Move CSS to Separate File:**
If your CSS isn't already in a separate file:
1. Create **css/styles.css**
2. Cut all CSS code from `<style>` tags in HTML
3. Paste into styles.css
4. Link to CSS file in HTML head section:

```html
<head>
    <title>Your Website</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/styles.css">
</head>
```

**Move JavaScript to Separate File:**
1. Create **js/script.js**
2. Cut all JavaScript code from `<script>` tags in HTML
3. Paste into script.js
4. Link to JavaScript file before closing body tag:

```html
<body>
    <!-- Your HTML content -->
    <script src="js/script.js"></script>
</body>
```

**Step 2: Create Consistent Header and Navigation**

**Header HTML Structure:**
Add this header to the top of your current HTML (after `<body>` tag):

```html
<header>
    <div class="header-container">
        <h1 class="site-title">Bryce MacRae</h1>
        <nav class="main-nav">
            <ul>
                <li><a href="index.html" class="nav-link">Home</a></li>
                <li><a href="about.html" class="nav-link">About</a></li>
                <li><a href="projects.html" class="nav-link">Projects</a></li>
                <li><a href="contact.html" class="nav-link">Contact</a></li>
            </ul>
        </nav>
    </div>
</header>
```

**Header CSS Styling:**
Add this to your **css/styles.css** file:

```css
/* Header Styles */
header {
    background-color: #333;
    color: white;
    padding: 1rem 0;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 100;
}

.header-container {
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 20px;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
}

.site-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.8rem;
}

.main-nav ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    gap: 2rem;
}

.nav-link {
    color: white;
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
}

.nav-link:hover {
    background-color: #555;
}

.nav-link.active {
    background-color: #007bff;
}
```

**Step 3: Create Consistent Footer**

**Footer HTML Structure:**
Add this footer before the closing `</body>` tag:

```html
